SYNOPSIS
        void async_save_object(string file, closure callback)
        void async_save_object(string file, closure callback, int format)

DESCRIPTION
        Capture this object's persistent variables and queue an atomic
        background file replacement. Requires --enable-use-async-io.

        The format, default version, excluded nosave/static variables,
        supported values, shared references and cycles follow save_object().
        An omitted format or -1 selects the current save format. Supported
        explicit versions are 0 through 3. Lightweight objects are unsupported.

        The master valid_write() operation is "save_object". Path handling
        matches save_object(): a trailing .c is stripped, then .o is appended.
        Supplying "player.o" therefore writes "player.o.o".

        Serialization completes during this call. Accepted immutable bytes
        retain the captured values when the source is subsequently modified
        or destroyed. Only filesystem work runs in the helper. This reduces
        blocking file I/O, but does not move serialization CPU time away from
        the game loop. The serializer uses a bounded output sink rather than
        constructing an additional complete save string.

        callback receives 0 after the complete save has been written, closed
        successfully and renamed into place, or -1 on failure. It never runs
        inline. Admission, permission, invalid format, serialization, encoding
        and capacity errors are synchronous and do not arrange a callback.
        Nested save serialization is rejected while a serializer is active,
        including reentry through a Python __save__ hook. Permission hooks
        may submit saves before serialization starts.

        The helper creates a unique exclusive temporary file in the target
        directory, writes and closes it, then renames it over the destination.
        Failure before rename preserves the old destination. New saves use
        mode 0640, restricted by the inherited umask. The old destination is
        never unlinked first. If the helper loses its reply after rename, a
        reported failure can have an uncertain committed result; there is no
        automatic retry. Completion is not a power-loss durability guarantee.

        A helper failure before its temporary-file identity is acknowledged,
        or failure to obtain that identity, can leave an unannounced temporary
        file. Known temporary files are cleaned only after verifying their
        identity; unrelated files are never removed by guessing a filename.

        Queue bounds, callback context, FIFO ordering and shutdown behavior
        are shared with async_write(). Wait for this callback before calling
        restore_object() or synchronously replacing the same save path.

EXAMPLES
        void saved(int result)
        {
            if (result) debug_message("Player save failed.\n");
        }

        async_save_object("save/player", #'saved);

